## Use Case for eu-ehds:A10
Description: This example demonstrates the exercise of the eu-ehds:A10 right, representing a fundamental control mechanism for natural persons within the 
primary use of health data. It records a use case where a dpv:DataSubject opts out from having their electronic health records made available 
through national or cross-border access services. By creating a dpv:RightExerciseRecord, the framework provides a machine-readable instruction 
to the dpv:DataController (the National Health Portal). This record ensures that health professionals outside of those currently treating the 
patient cannot retrieve the records through the EHDS infrastructure, thereby fulfilling the patient's right to digital self-determination under 
EHDS Article 10.

## Use Case for eu-ehds:A9
Description: This example illustrates the implementation of the eu-ehds:A9 right as a requirement of a health data exchange. 
It defines the dpv:PersonalDataHandling operations of the Primary Use EHR Retrieval Service as being inherently bound to transparency obligations. 
The dpv:PrivacyByDesign approach is defined which helps the model to demonstrate how the system satisfies EHDS Article 9 through an integrated 
dpv:TechnicalMeasure ex:AutomaticAccessNotifier. This backend service ensures that every instance of professional access triggers a real-time 
notification to the data subject. The inclusion of dpv:hasComplianceStatus dpv:Compliant asserts that the service is built to meet regulatory
standards by default, transforming a legal right into an automated feature that ensures continuous accountability in healthcare data processing.

## Use Case for eu-ehds:21
Description: This example illustrates the exercise of the eu-ehds:A21 right, representing the formal procedural mechanism for natural or legal 
persons to lodge a complaint when their digital health rights are compromised. The use case begins shows where a dpv:DataSubject encounters a 
persistent obstruction while attempting to exercise their right to access electronic health data (eu-ehds:A3) through a national portal. Rather 
than being a simple administrative log, the model captures the transition from a perceived violation to a regulatory process by linking the 
dpv:RightExerciseRecord to a specific dpv:hasImpact. By utilising the logic of rights impact assessment, the application of eu-ehds:A21 provides 
a machine-readable justification for the complaint, specifically identifying the incident as a risk:RightsViolated instance where access was 
obstructed. The description identifies the National Digital Health Authority as the dpv:Recipient, establishing it as the competent body 
responsible for oversight under the EHDS framework. This structured approach ensures that the complaint is not merely a text entry but a 
functional piece of metadata that connects the failure of a technical service to a formal legal process, providing the transparency and 
auditability required by EHDS Article 21.

